    "narrative": "The evidence was mixed: delivery times in the north region did worsen, but the run did not support treating that as clear service-quality degradation on its own. Rising order volume was supported as a contributor to the slower deliveries.\n\nThe first claim was weakened: there were 3 supporting evidence items and 1 refuting evidence item for the idea that average delivery days increased in a way that indicated degrading service quality. The second claim was supported: it had 5 supporting evidence items and no refuting evidence for the explanation that rising order volume was contributing to the delay.\n\nThe run used 3 experiments: two time-series trend analyses and a change-point detection step. It stopped because there was sufficient evidence for a mixed conclusion, but 2 open questions remained: whether the delivery-time increase was sustained or just a one-off shift, and whether staff_count or on_time_rate would separate service degradation from volume-driven delay. The dataset had 96 rows.",
